From: Erfan Abdi <erfangplus@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] apklayer: guest thread lifetime: a signal handler that attaches a thread no longer calls malloc; no stale locks in a forked child; no read of an unknown record

The thread stress (tests/apps/hello-arm64/threads.c: 2000 threads created, joined from other threads, detached, pthread_exit, signalled while exiting, with a
fork in between) hung with libberberis_arm64 aac5755a: a thread that got a guest signal (pthread_kill of a thread that was starting or exiting) sat in
`__tls_get_module -> malloc` under the arena lock it already held. The signal handler's first access of the library's thread_local
(g_handle_host_signal_depth) made the library's dynamic TLS block (the host's lazy __tls_get_addr, malloc 2.2 KB) while the interrupted code was in malloc.
Everything a handler that attaches a thread runs is now free of malloc: the depth is a GuestThread member; AttachCurrentThread does not call
pthread_getattr_np (glibc allocates a cpuset; and its attr was never destroyed) from a signal handler; the default signal actions' shared_ptr is made once
(a control block allocates); the table of 0029/0038 (record -> host pthread_t) is a fixed array (no unordered_map/deque), and a thread attached only for a
signal handler or a key destructor keeps no record. The child of a fork gets fresh locks for that table and the thread map (a lock another thread held at the
fork stays locked for ever) and its record's tid (the guest's gettid() answered the parent's tid: tgkill(getpid(), gettid(), sig) in a child hit nothing). GuestPthreadToHost no longer reads the tid word of a pointer that is not a registered record (an evicted record's memory is
unmapped: the read crashed).

